WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.600
Scientists purified a protein that made normal cells grow like tumour cells.

2
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.400
They named it transforming growth factor beta.

3
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.060
Then the same protein was caught holding other cells back.

4
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.980
What does TGF-β do to a cell once it lands?

5
00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:26.660
Follow its message from the membrane to a gene.

6
00:00:27.433 --> 00:00:32.133
The receptors for TGF-β are kinases of two types.

7
00:00:32.873 --> 00:00:36.773
The type II kinase is active before any ligand arrives.

8
00:00:37.793 --> 00:00:41.353
TGF-β is a dimer, two chains joined together.

9
00:00:41.993 --> 00:00:44.253
It binds two type II receptors.

10
00:00:45.293 --> 00:00:49.973
Then the ligand and type II together recruit two type I receptors.

11
00:00:50.913 --> 00:00:53.893
Four receptor chains now surround one ligand.

12
00:00:54.717 --> 00:01:02.057
Type II adds phosphate to a stretch of type I just inside the membrane, called the GS

13
00:01:02.057 --> 00:01:09.077
domain. At rest, the GS domain holds the type I kinase in an inactive shape.

14
00:01:10.097 --> 00:01:11.757
Phosphate ends that arrangement.

15
00:01:12.537 --> 00:01:17.117
The kinase turns on, and the stretch becomes a docking site for a Smad.

16
00:01:18.197 --> 00:01:22.397
Think of type II as a lit match, and type I as a fuse.

17
00:01:22.397 --> 00:01:26.237
The ligand only holds the match to the fuse.

18
00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:31.227
The active type I kinase phosphorylates a Smad.

19
00:01:31.807 --> 00:01:36.647
A Smad is itself a transcription factor, a protein that binds DNA.

20
00:01:37.907 --> 00:01:44.127
TGF-β receptors phosphorylate Smad2 and Smad3, on two serines at the end of

21
00:01:44.127 --> 00:01:49.027
the tail. A phosphorylated tail fits a pocket on a neighbouring Smad.

22
00:01:49.807 --> 00:01:54.507
So the Smads leave the receptor, and typically two of them join one Smad4.

23
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:59.500
The complex collects in the nucleus and binds DNA.

24
00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:05.240
But it reads a sequence only four base pairs long, and holds it weakly.

25
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:11.560
That sequence turns up millions of times in a human genome, so a Smad complex alone

26
00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:12.860
cannot pick a gene.

27
00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:19.000
It binds firmly only next to partner proteins, and each cell type makes its own

28
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:26.380
partners. In many epithelial cells, the partners turn on p15 and p21, and the cell

29
00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:31.720
stops dividing. In a fibroblast, the same pathway turns on collagen genes.

30
00:02:32.633 --> 00:02:35.793
The ligand brings two kinases together.

31
00:02:36.653 --> 00:02:42.013
Type II phosphorylates type I, and type I phosphorylates a Smad.

32
00:02:42.873 --> 00:02:44.833
The phosphate runs one way.

33
00:02:45.653 --> 00:02:49.933
In each cell, the partners decide what the message of the Smad means.

34
00:02:50.773 --> 00:02:55.273
Keep this rule: the ligand only introduces two kinases.
